  "ai_language_tip": "Speak polite Arabic close to Modern Standard but with common spoken words; Levantine dialect is understood across lb/jo/sy/ps, Iraqi dialect differs (iq). English is common in Amman and Beirut businesses; French is common in Lebanon; Kurdish is used in the Kurdistan Region of Iraq.",
  "english_ok": "sometimes — Common in hotels, clinics and restaurants in Amman and Beirut, and with younger staff; limited in Syria, much of Iraq and smaller towns.",
  "politeness": "Greetings matter: open with 'Marhaba' / 'As-salamu alaykum' and a short 'how are you'; use 'ustaz' / 'madame' or 'hajj' / 'hajjeh' for older people; requests are soft and friendly; close with thanks and a blessing.",
  "name_tip": "Bookings under first name or family name (often with 'Abu/Umm + child's name' informally); Latin spellings of Arabic names vary (Mohammad/Muhammad), so spell slowly or send by WhatsApp.",
  "phone_tip": "lb 0X XXX XXX or 7X XXX XXX (+961, e.g. 03 12345678); jo 07 9XXX XXXX (+962); sy 09XX XXX XXX (+963); ps 059/056 XXX XXXX (+970); iq 07XX XXX XXXX (+964); WhatsApp is the default for confirmations everywhere.",
  "time_tip": "Spoken 12h with part of day ('7 بالليل', 'الصبح'); 'سبعة ونص' = 7:30, 'تمانية إلا ربع' = 7:45 (no trap); confirm am/pm; Ramadan shifts restaurant hours to after sunset (iftar).",
  "number_tip": "Western digits and Arabic-Indic digits (٠ ١ ٢ ٣) both appear; numbers usually read in pairs; in chat, Arabizi uses digits for letters (3 = ع, 7 = ح, 2 = ء), so a '3' or '7' inside a word is not a number.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid Friday midday prayer, late afternoon nap time at small businesses, and calls after 22:00; during Ramadan avoid the hour before iftar; Eid holidays close most businesses for several days.",
  "closed_days": "Weekend is Friday–Saturday in jo, sy, ps and iq; Saturday–Sunday in lb (with short Friday in the public sector); Christian-owned shops may close Sunday and Muslim-owned shops Friday; Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha close most businesses; many extra religious holidays in lb.",
  "booking_channels": "Phone and WhatsApp dominate for restaurants, clinics, salons and trades; some Amman/Beirut venues use web reservation widgets; Talabat, Careem and local apps for delivery; Careem, Uber or local apps for rides in jo/lb/iq; clinic booking apps exist but phone calls to the clinic secretary are the norm.",
  "voice_menu": "Telecoms (Zain, Orange, Alfa, touch, Jawwal, Asiacell), banks and utilities use IVR; restaurants, clinics and shops usually answer directly, often on a mobile.",
  "callback_habits": "Businesses confirm or remind via WhatsApp message or a call on the day; clinic secretaries call back to fix a slot.",
